---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="03-loops"
  lessonSlug="04-collatz"
  hook="The textbook's own §7.3 example as a graded problem. The math is unsolved; the program is straightforward."
>
  <h2>The rule</h2>
  <p>
    Start with a positive integer <code>n</code>. Each step:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>If <code>n</code> is even → <code>n = n // 2</code></li>
    <li>If <code>n</code> is odd  → <code>n = 3 * n + 1</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Stop when <code>n == 1</code>. Count steps.</p>

  <Callout tone="warning" title="🔒 no_paste mode is ON">
    <p>
      Graded homework. Paste is blocked in the entry file — type it out
      yourself. The Tutor maxes out at hint level 2.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Worked trace — <code>n = 3</code></h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`# step 0: n = 3  (odd)  → 3*3 + 1 = 10
# step 1: n = 10 (even) → 10 // 2  = 5
# step 2: n = 5  (odd)  → 3*5 + 1 = 16
# step 3: n = 16 (even) → 16 // 2  = 8
# step 4: n = 8  (even) → 8  // 2  = 4
# step 5: n = 4  (even) → 4  // 2  = 2
# step 6: n = 2  (even) → 2  // 2  = 1
# step 7: n = 1  STOP

# Let's verify with code:
def collatz_steps(n):
    steps = 0
    while n != 1:
        if n % 2 == 0:
            n = n // 2
        else:
            n = 3 * n + 1
        steps += 1
    return steps

print(collatz_steps(3))     # → 7
print(collatz_steps(27))    # → 111   (the famous case)`}
  />

  <h2>The integer-vs-float trap</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="Why // matters"
    code={`# Wrong:
n = 4
n = n / 2          # n is now 2.0 (a float)
print(n, type(n))

# Right:
n = 4
n = n // 2         # n is now 2 (an int)
print(n, type(n))`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="Why the / version is dangerous">
    <p>
      With <code>/</code>, <code>n</code> becomes a float. The condition
      <code>n != 1</code> may behave unpredictably as rounding accumulates.
      For some inputs you get infinite loops; for others you get
      off-by-something step counts. The test <code>test_returns_int_not_float</code>
      catches this. The classic <code>test_n_27_the_famous_one</code> times
      out if you use <code>/</code>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Why n = 27 is famous</h2>
  <p>
    The sequence takes <strong>111 steps</strong> and reaches a peak of
    <strong>9232</strong> before coming down. Yet 27 isn't special — the
    sequence is just chaotic. From Think Python §7.3:
    <em> "So far, no one has been able to prove [it terminates for all positive
    n] or disprove it."</em>
  </p>

  <h2>Acceptance criteria</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>All 7 tests pass</li>
    <li>Uses a <code>while</code> loop (not recursion)</li>
    <li>Uses <code>//</code> for the even step (not <code>/</code>)</li>
    <li>Returns an <code>int</code></li>
  </ul>
</Lecture>
